-- title:
--   Frobenius acting as an integer descends all endomorphisms
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a finite field, let $k$ be an algebraically closed field that is an $F$-algebra, and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $F$ satisfying `W.IsElliptic`. Let $\sigma : k \to k$ be an $F$-algebra homomorphism with $\sigma x = x^{\#F}$ for every $x \in k$, and let $a$ be an integer such that the map induced by $\sigma$ on the points of the base change $W\!\restriction_k$ satisfies $\mathrm{map}\,\sigma\,P = a \cdot P$ for every $P \in (W\!\restriction_k)(k)$. The conclusion is an equality of two subrings of the ring of additive endomorphisms of the group of $k$-points of $W\!\restriction_k$: the subring `rationalEndSubring k (W.baseChange k)`, generated by those additive endomorphisms that are either zero or satisfy `IsRationallyRepresented k (W.baseChange k) (W.baseChange k)` (rational representability with coefficients in $k$), coincides with `rationalEndSubring k W`, generated by those that are either zero or satisfy `IsRationallyRepresented k W W` (rational representability with coefficients in $F$). Both sides make sense as subrings of the same ring because base changing a curve over $k$ along the identity of $k$ returns the same curve.
--
--   This is the step in Deuring's theory of endomorphism rings in which, once a power of Frobenius acts on the geometric points as an integer, every geometric endomorphism of the curve is already defined over the finite base field, so that the geometric and the $F$-rational endomorphism rings agree. It is used in [`WeierstrassCurve.exists_subfield_model_frobenius_eq_smul_rationalEndSubring_equiv`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_subfield_model_frobenius_eq_smul_rationalEndSubring_equiv), where the base field is enlarged until all endomorphisms become rational over it.
